import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Privacy: Fudge" description="Every network call Fudge can make, and what stays on your machine.">
  <article class="page">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lead">Fudge runs on your machine. What you type, your history and your files never leave it. There is no account, no telemetry and no analytics.</p>
    <p class="fine">This page mirrors <code>PRIVACY.md</code> in the repository. Fudge is pre-alpha, so this describes intended behaviour.</p>

    <h2>Network calls</h2>
    <p>These are all of them.</p>
    <h3>Currency rates</h3>
    <p>When you convert currency, at most once an hour. Fudge asks <code>open.er-api.com</code> for today's USD rates, the same request for every user. Your amounts and currencies stay local. Turn it off with <code>network_features = false</code> in the config, or in Settings.</p>
    <h3>Update check</h3>
    <p>Off by default. If you turn it on, Fudge asks GitHub for the latest release.</p>
    <h3>Plugin registry</h3>
    <p>Only when you browse or install a plugin.</p>
    <p>Web searches you start with <code>/online</code>, <code>/yt</code> or <code>/gh</code> open in your browser. Fudge never searches the web on its own. Like any web request, these reveal your IP address to the server that answers.</p>

    <h2>What stays on your machine</h2>
    <h3>History</h3>
    <p>The results you run, used to rank them. Stored in <code>$XDG_DATA_HOME/fudge/history.sqlite</code> on Linux and <code>~/Library/Application Support/Fudge/</code> on macOS. Window switches and web searches are never stored. You can turn history off and clear it.</p>
    <h3>File index</h3>
    <p>File names, sizes and dates. Never file contents. Content search uses your system's own indexer.</p>
    <h3>Audit log</h3>
    <p>Each <code>&gt;</code> shell command and each confirmed irreversible action, such as ending a process.</p>
    <p>Delete those folders to remove everything.</p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>No analytics, no web fonts, no CDN, and no request to any third party. The home page counts this live. The demo's currency answers use fixed sample rates and never touch the network.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
